          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.638,
            0.085,
            0.887,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.224,
                0.208,
                0.773,
                0.239
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.28: A load being lifted up (a) vertically, (b) along an inclined plane, and (c) along an inclined plane of larger length"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.242,
            0.888,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "An **inclined plane** is a simple machine that helps move a heavy load to a higher (or lower) level. Let us now find its mechanical advantage."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.056,
            0.368,
            0.179,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": "The work done, that is the product of force and displacement, is the same in all cases. If the force decreases, the displacement increases, thereby the work done remains constant."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.28,
            0.889,
            0.349
          ],
          "content": "Let the mass of the object be $m$ . Then, the load is the weight mg of the object to be lifted. Let $F ^ { \\prime }$ be the force required to raise the object to a height h (Fig. 7.28a). Then, $F ^ { \\prime }$ is the effort. Let the length of the inclined plane be L (Fig. 7.28b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.062,
            0.35,
            0.121,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": "**Note**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.35,
            0.889,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": "If you move the object at constant speed up the inclined plane, then using Eq. (7.2),"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.355,
            0.384,
            0.759,
            0.403
          ],
          "content": "total work done by you on the object $= F ^ { \\prime } \\times L$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.405,
            0.401,
            0.424
          ],
          "content": "And using Eq. (7.8),"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.358,
            0.424,
            0.755,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": "potential energy gained by the object = mgh"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.444,
            0.888,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "Thus, from the work-energy theorem (Eq. 7.3), we obtain (ignoring friction)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.496,
            0.465,
            0.619,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": "F^{\\prime} \\times L = m g h",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.496,
            0.485,
            0.615,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{mg}{F^{\\prime}} = \\frac{L}{h}",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.521,
            0.835,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": "Now, mg is the load and $F ^ { \\prime }$ is the effort, so using Eq. (7.12), we obtain"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.337,
            0.541,
            0.887,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{mechanism\\;advantage} = \\frac{\\mathrm{l\\;oad}}{\\mathrm{effort}} = \\frac{mg}{F'} = \\frac{L}{h}\\tag{7.13}",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.582,
            0.89,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "Since L is larger than $h ,$ the force $F ^ { \\prime }$ is less than mg and the mechanical advantage of inclined plane is greater than 1. By further increasing $L ,$ for example, by making the inclined plane longer with a shallower angle (Fig. 7.28c), we can further reduce the effort $F ^ { \\prime }$ required to move the object."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.045,
            0.661,
            0.215,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.654,
            0.89,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7.12:** A person uses an inclined ramp to raise an object over a step 30 cm high. The ramp has a width of 40 cm. What is the mechanical advantage of the ramp that helps the person achieve the task?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.708,
            0.889,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "**Answer:** As shown in Fig. 7.29, when the height AB is 30 cm and the width BC is 40 cm, the length AC of the ramp is 50 cm (right-angled triangle property)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.746,
            0.787,
            0.783
          ],
          "content": "Using Eq. (7.13),   mechanical advantage $\\frac{L}{h}=\\frac{50\\  cm }{30\\  cm }=1.67$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.244,
            0.783,
            0.307,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.31,
            0.789,
            0.501,
            0.809
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.259,
            0.82,
            0.744,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "9. Explain why roads on hills are built to wind around in gentle slopes rather than going straight up (Fig. 4.26)?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.855,
            0.744,
            0.905
          ],
          "content": "10. To reach a higher floor, we find climbing an inclined ladder easier in comparison to climbing a vertical ladder (Fig. 7.30). Explain why."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.75,
